1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,253
Now graphical E D A for numerical variables.

2
00:00:03,253 --> 00:00:05,545
Plot type one is the histogram.

3
00:00:05,545 --> 00:00:14,492
It splits the range into bins and counts how many rows fall into each, the most direct picture of a distribution's shape.

4
00:00:14,492 --> 00:00:16,636
Tall bars mean common values.

5
00:00:16,636 --> 00:00:21,960
It is the best way to take a first, honest look at any numerical column.

6
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:29,686
Order quantity and discount show flat, evenly spread bars, visually confirming their near-zero skewness.

7
00:00:29,686 --> 00:00:39,542
Sales, unit price and shipping cost show one towering bar near zero and an almost invisible tail, the picture behind their extreme skewness numbers.

8
00:00:39,542 --> 00:00:48,200
And profit shows a sharp spike near zero with real mass on both the negative and positive side, confirming the loss-making orders.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:53,728
Plot type two is the density plot, built with kernel density estimation.

10
00:00:53,728 --> 00:00:57,865
It is a smoothed histogram, a continuous curve instead of bars.

11
00:00:57,865 --> 00:01:09,160
It removes the arbitrary choice of bin width and gives a cleaner picture, but smoothing can hide small real spikes that a histogram shows, so the two complement each other.

12
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:14,912
Order quantity's curve confirms a genuinely flat, near-uniform shape.

13
00:01:14,912 --> 00:01:22,592
Profit shows a sharp, narrow peak straddling zero, distinct from the one-sided skew of sales or unit price.

14
00:01:22,592 --> 00:01:27,975
Sales and unit price again collapse into a flat line squashed against zero.

15
00:01:27,975 --> 00:01:32,712
Density plots handle moderate skew well, but not this level of it.

16
00:01:33,512 --> 00:01:35,734
Plot type three is the boxplot.

17
00:01:35,734 --> 00:01:38,529
It packs five numbers into one picture:

18
00:01:38,529 --> 00:01:44,692
minimum, Q one, median, Q three and maximum, plus individually plotted outlier points.

19
00:01:44,692 --> 00:01:52,862
The box spans the middle fifty percent, whiskers show the normal range, and dots beyond them are flagged outliers.

20
00:01:52,862 --> 00:01:59,384
It is best for comparing spread and spotting outliers across several features side by side.

21
00:02:00,184 --> 00:02:06,224
Order quantity and discount have honest, well-formed boxes with essentially no outliers.

22
00:02:06,224 --> 00:02:14,597
Profit's box straddles zero with outlier dots stretching both ways, large losses on one side and large gains on the other.

23
00:02:14,597 --> 00:02:22,216
Sales, unit price and shipping cost have boxes crushed toward zero, exactly as their dispersion numbers warned.

24
00:02:23,016 --> 00:02:25,349
Plot type four is the violin plot.

25
00:02:25,349 --> 00:02:34,888
It mirrors a density curve on both sides of a central boxplot, so you see the full distribution shape and the five-number summary together.

26
00:02:34,888 --> 00:02:42,025
A boxplot alone can hide whether data is single-peaked, multi-peaked or lopsided; the violin reveals it.

27
00:02:42,025 --> 00:02:45,456
Use it when the shape itself is part of the story.

28
00:02:46,256 --> 00:02:54,132
Order quantity has the most rectangular, even violin body, the clearest visual confirmation of a uniform shape.

29
00:02:54,132 --> 00:03:01,583
Profit's violin is narrow and symmetric around zero, unlike the one-sided needles of sales or unit price.

30
00:03:01,583 --> 00:03:06,196
Sales, unit price and shipping cost appear as needle-thin spikes.

31
00:03:06,196 --> 00:03:12,440
The violin plot cannot rescue a shape this extreme any more than the density plot could.

32
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:18,261
Plot type five overlays the same feature's density curve for several categories.

33
00:03:18,261 --> 00:03:26,419
Curves that overlap heavily mean the category barely affects the feature; curves that separate mean the category is a real driver.

34
00:03:26,419 --> 00:03:32,632
It is an early visual check of whether a categorical feature is worth investigating as a predictor.

35
00:03:33,432 --> 00:03:38,505
All three categories centre close to zero, with gains and losses in each.

36
00:03:38,505 --> 00:03:43,439
Technology shows a visibly wider spread, bigger wins and bigger losses.

37
00:03:43,439 --> 00:03:45,315
Average profit confirms it:

38
00:03:45,315 --> 00:03:53,376
technology averages four hundred twenty nine dollars per order, more than six times furniture's sixty eight dollars.

39
00:03:54,176 --> 00:03:59,243
Delivery truck shows the widest spread and highest typical shipping cost.

40
00:03:59,243 --> 00:04:05,142
Regular air and express air cluster at lower costs, with express air slightly higher.

41
00:04:05,142 --> 00:04:07,016
This is a sensible pattern:

42
00:04:07,016 --> 00:04:12,152
heavier or bulkier shipments that need truck delivery naturally cost more.

43
00:04:12,952 --> 00:04:15,089
Now compare the two approaches.

44
00:04:15,089 --> 00:04:26,604
For unit price, the non-graphical view said skewness fourteen, kurtosis two seventy one, mean more than four times the median, ten percent of rows flagged as outliers.

45
00:04:26,604 --> 00:04:36,877
The graphical view showed one towering bar, a boxplot crushed flat with a wall of outlier dots past sixty seven hundred dollars, and a needle violin.

46
00:04:36,877 --> 00:04:37,497
Combined:

47
00:04:37,497 --> 00:04:44,392
most items are inexpensive, but a small set of high-end products pulls every average sharply upward.

48
00:04:45,192 --> 00:04:56,804
For order quantity, skewness is about zero, mean and median sit almost on top of each other, kurtosis is negative one point two one, and zero percent are outliers.

49
00:04:56,804 --> 00:05:05,210
The histogram is rectangular across one to fifty, the boxplot has no outlier dots, and the violin is the most uniform.

50
00:05:05,210 --> 00:05:09,912
The number and the picture agree closely, a rare, reassuring case.

51
00:05:10,712 --> 00:05:12,054
The overall lesson.

52
00:05:12,054 --> 00:05:17,138
When they agree, as with order quantity, you can trust either technique.

53
00:05:17,138 --> 00:05:30,061
When they diverge in detail, as with unit price, the skewness number warned of extreme skew, but only the boxplot showed that the entire spread lives in the outlier dots, not the box.

54
00:05:30,061 --> 00:05:37,123
Non-graphical statistics are fast and precise but abstract; plots are intuitive but can hide detail.

55
00:05:37,123 --> 00:05:38,888
Always use both together.

56
00:05:39,688 --> 00:05:41,531
Now categorical variables.

57
00:05:41,531 --> 00:05:44,153
Plot type six is the frequency table:

58
00:05:44,153 --> 00:05:49,893
a count and percentage for each category, the categorical equivalent of describe.

59
00:05:49,893 --> 00:05:58,468
The biggest number is the most common category, and a category with a tiny count is a warning for later grouped analysis.

60
00:05:58,468 --> 00:06:02,224
It is the very first check on any categorical column.

61
00:06:03,024 --> 00:06:04,223
Product category:

62
00:06:04,223 --> 00:06:13,462
office supplies is the majority, four thousand six hundred ten orders, fifty five percent; furniture is smallest at twenty percent.

63
00:06:13,462 --> 00:06:14,167
Ship mode:

64
00:06:14,167 --> 00:06:20,303
regular air dominates at seventy five percent; express air is rarest at twelve percent.

65
00:06:20,303 --> 00:06:21,502
Customer segment:

66
00:06:21,502 --> 00:06:27,144
corporate is largest at thirty seven percent, with the other three fairly close.

67
00:06:27,944 --> 00:06:30,195
Plot type seven is the bar plot.

68
00:06:30,195 --> 00:06:39,200
It turns a frequency table into a picture, one bar per category, so the eye compares heights far faster than numbers in a table.

69
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:47,360
It is best for presenting frequencies, or for comparing a summary statistic, like average profit, across categories.

70
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:52,642
Product category shows office supplies towering over the other two.

71
00:06:52,642 --> 00:06:58,997
Ship mode shows a steep drop after regular air, since most orders default to standard shipping.

72
00:06:58,997 --> 00:07:05,152
Customer segment shows a real but modest lead for corporate over three fairly even segments.

73
00:07:05,952 --> 00:07:10,051
Plot type eight is the pie chart, a part-to-whole view.

74
00:07:10,051 --> 00:07:10,797
A caution:

75
00:07:10,797 --> 00:07:18,325
pie charts get hard to read past four or five slices, and are poor at comparing similar-sized slices.

76
00:07:18,325 --> 00:07:22,424
Product category, with exactly three groups, fits well.

77
00:07:22,424 --> 00:07:27,120
A variable with eight categories is better shown as a bar plot.

78
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:32,137
Office supplies alone accounts for well over half of every order.

79
00:07:32,137 --> 00:07:37,781
Technology and furniture together make up less than half, despite being two categories.

80
00:07:37,781 --> 00:07:44,528
Seeing this part-to-whole makes the dominance of office supplies easier to feel than the bar plot alone.

81
00:07:45,328 --> 00:07:46,767
Finally, anomalies.

82
00:07:46,767 --> 00:07:47,448
The rule:

83
00:07:47,448 --> 00:07:57,974
compute Q one, Q three and the I Q R, and flag anything below Q one minus one point five I Q R, or above Q three plus one point five I Q R.

84
00:07:57,974 --> 00:08:01,382
This non-graphical rule has a graphical twin:

85
00:08:01,382 --> 00:08:05,395
the same fences are what a boxplot draws as whiskers.

86
00:08:05,395 --> 00:08:07,970
And not every outlier is an error:

87
00:08:07,970 --> 00:08:16,072
a twenty seven thousand dollar profit and a fourteen thousand dollar loss are both genuine business events.

88
00:08:16,872 --> 00:08:26,475
Profit has one thousand seven hundred four outliers, twenty percent of rows, the highest rate, capturing both large gains and large losses.

89
00:08:26,475 --> 00:08:32,209
Sales has twelve percent, shipping cost and unit price about ten to twelve percent.

90
00:08:32,209 --> 00:08:36,492
Discount has just three outliers, and order quantity has none.

91
00:08:36,492 --> 00:08:44,160
Profit's outliers are exactly the extreme wins and losses that help explain why over half of orders lose money.

92
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:45,573
To recap:

93
00:08:45,573 --> 00:08:56,204
we applied three non-graphical technique families and eight plot types to every feature, matching every result with its plot and every plot with its number.

94
00:08:56,204 --> 00:09:02,133
The techniques agree on order quantity and reinforce each other on the skewed features:

95
00:09:02,133 --> 00:09:07,858
the numbers flag that something is unusual, and the plots show exactly what and why.

96
00:09:07,858 --> 00:09:08,403
Next up:

97
00:09:08,403 --> 00:09:11,197
correlation, covariance and scatterplots.

98
00:09:11,197 --> 00:09:12,560
Thanks for watching.

